# Content-addressed inputs must produce the same bytes on every machine.
#
# `fixtures/trials/**` is pinned by `scripts/seed-content.mjs`, whose keccak digest is posted
# on-chain as a trial's spec/tests hash. With `core.autocrlf=true` (the default on Windows
# developer boxes, including this repository's) a checkout handed those files CRLF line endings
# while the committed blob stayed LF - so the same commit produced two different digests
# depending on the machine that read the file. A digest posted from one box would not resolve to
# content on another, which is the one thing a commitment must never do.
#
# `text eol=lf` makes the working tree byte-identical to the blob. Scoped to the fixtures on
# purpose: this is only where a file's bytes become a hash somebody else has to reproduce.
fixtures/** text eol=lf

# The edge runs on Linux. A CRLF in a shell script is a runtime error there
# ("\r: command not found"), and a CRLF in a Caddyfile or an nginx map is a
# parse error. Pin these to LF so the line ending cannot become a production
# incident on the way to the box.
deploy/edge/** text eol=lf
